> Regarding your first point, I understand that a t-test omits individuals with missing genotypes while most of the methods in scanone impute missing genotypes. However, I am using method="mr", which also omits individuals with missing genotypes, in the same way that a t-test does, right?

Another potential difference is that scanone with method="mr" assumes constant variance in the two genotype groups.  You can get that with t.test with argument var.equal=TRUE

  data(hyper)
  hyper <- hyper[1,] # pull out chr 1
  out.mr <http://out.mr> <- scanone(hyper, method="mr")
  out.t <- apply(hyper$geno[[1]]$data, 2,
                 function(a,b) t.test(b ~ factor(a), var.equal=TRUE)$p.value,
                 hyper$pheno[,1])
  plot(out.mr <http://out.mr>[,3], -log10(out.t))

karl

